[Chapter "Giuoco Pianissimo (3...Bc5)"]
[Line "Against the ...d5 break"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Classical Variation, Giuoco Pianissimo"]

{Black tries to free the position with ...d5. Take, then hit e5.} 1. e4 {Take the centre and open lines for the queen and the f1 bishop.} 1... e5 2. Nf3 {Develop with an attack on e5.} 2... Nc6 3. Bc4 {The Italian: the bishop aims at f7, the weakest square in Black's camp.} 3... Bc5 4. c3 {Prepare d4 for later, and give the bishop a retreat square on b3.} 4... Nf6 5. d3 {The modern, slow approach (Giuoco Pianissimo): keep e4 solid and build up before opening the centre.} 5... O-O 6. O-O {Castle.} 6... d5 7. exd5 {Take, so Black has to recapture with a piece.} 7... Nxd5 8. Re1 {Hit e5: with the d-pawns gone, only the knight defends it.} *
